Linear search is the simplest searching algorithm. It checks each element in a list one by one, from the beginning, until it finds the target value or reaches the end of the list. If the target is found, the search stops and returns the index. If not, it returns a sentinel value such as -1. It works on both sorted and unsorted data, but it is not efficient for very large datasets because its worst-case time complexity is O(n). Here is a basic implementation:

```python
def linear_search(arr, target):
    for i in range(len(arr)):
        if arr[i] == target:
            return i
    return -1

my_list = [10, 20, 30, 40]
print(linear_search(my_list, 30))  # Output: 2
```

