micro_lesson.content:
Linear search has a worst-case time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of elements, because it may inspect every element before concluding the target is absent. The best case is O(1) when the target is at index 0. A common runtime misconception is that binary search applies to unsorted lists; it does not. Another pitfall is assuming the average case is always O(n/2) and therefore negligible; constants matter in practice for small inputs. When analyzing runtime, focus on the number of comparisons in the worst case. Here is a reference implementation:

```python
def linear_search(arr, target):
    for i in range(len(arr)):
        if arr[i] == target:
            return i
    return -1
```

In the worst case, the loop runs `len(arr)` times, giving O(n). If the list is empty, the loop body never executes, yielding O(1) best case for that input.

